Specifically, if \(m = m_0 + m_1 p + \cdots + m_s p^s\) and \(k = k_0 + k_1 p + \cdots + k_s p^s\), then: \[
\binom{m}{k} \equiv \prod_{i=0}^s \binom{m_i}{k_i} \pmod{p}
\]

Since we're dealing with odd coefficients, which are modulo 2, Lucas' Theorem simplifies to: \[
\binom{m}{k} \equiv \prod_{i=0}^s \binom{m_i}{k_i} \pmod{2}
\]
